On Windows, `stop()` always uses `taskkill /T /F` first. This bypasses any graceful shutdown path the process might have and can be harsher than necessary. Consider attempting a normal `proc.kill()` (or `SIGTERM`) first with a short timeout, then escalating to `taskkill /T /F` only if the process hasn’t exited; also consider setting `stdio: "ignore"` on the `taskkill` child to avoid holding open pipes unnecessarily (see similar usage in `packages/opencode/src/shell/shell.ts`).
```suggestion
    if (process.platform === "win32") {
      proc.kill()

      const exited = await Promise.race([
        proc.exited.then(() => true),
        new Promise<boolean>((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve(false), 250)),
      ])
      if (exited) return

      if (proc.pid) {
        const task = launch("taskkill", ["/pid", String(proc.pid), "/T", "/F"], {
          windowsHide: true,
          stdio: "ignore",
        })
        const code = await new Promise<number>((resolve) => {
          task.once("exit", (code, signal) => resolve(code ?? (signal ? 1 : 0)))
          task.once("error", () => resolve(1))
        })
        if (code === 0) {
          await proc.exited
          return
        }
      }
    } else {
      proc.kill()
    }
```

`inject()` ignores non-2xx responses and never surfaces failures to the user. This can make the UI appear to succeed when the server rejected the injection.

Suggestion: check `res.ok`, display `res.text()`/status in `setError(...)` on failure, and optionally trigger a sync of the parent session so the injected message appears immediately if SSE delivery is delayed.
```suggestion
    try {
      const res = await (sdk.fetch ?? fetch)(`${base()}/sidekick/inject`, {
        method: "POST",
        headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
        body: JSON.stringify({ text }),
      })

      if (!res.ok) {
        const msg = await res.text().catch(() => res.statusText)
        setError(msg || `Failed to inject message (${res.status})`)
        return
      }

      setError(undefined)
      await sync.session.sync(props.parentID)
    } catch (e) {
      setError(e instanceof Error ? e.message : "Failed to inject message")
    }
```

These Sidekick API calls use raw `fetch` against `sdk.url` without including the SDK’s default request metadata (e.g. `x-opencode-directory` / workspace scoping headers or query params configured in `createOpencodeClient`). On the server, missing `directory`/header falls back to `process.cwd()`, which can target the wrong project/worktree.

Suggestion: use the generated SDK client for these endpoints (e.g. `sdk.client.session.sidekick.*`) or at minimum forward the same directory/workspace headers/query that the SDK uses so routing stays consistent.
